Emerging AI infrastructure increasingly relies on disaggregated memory fabrics, heterogeneous accelerators, chiplets, and CXL/UCIe interconnects. While confidential computing protects software execution through attestation, hardware components participating in AI execution often remain outside traditional trust boundaries. As AI systems become increasingly modular, this creates a growing gap between software-level trust and the integrity of the underlying hardware fabric. This position paper argues that hardware assurance should become a first-class primitive for disaggregated AI infrastructure. We propose a fabric-native trust model spanning silicon integrity, operational custody and tamper evidence, and lifecycle-aware hardware trust. We further introduce the concept of a Fabric Assurance Manifest, which extends processor-centric attestation with verifiable integrity evidence for heterogeneous hardware components. We outline key research challenges toward integrating hardware assurance into future AI infrastructure.